1
00:00:01,280 --> 00:00:14,090
 Convention was signed outlawing the slave trade, but...

2
00:00:14,090 --> 00:00:19,950
 A ship has just dropped anchor, Great King.

3
00:00:20,230 --> 00:00:23,770
 That's fine, that's fine. This ship wouldn't be Portuguese, English, or French.

4
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:26,480
 It's under English colors, Great King.

5
00:00:26,970 --> 00:00:30,100
 Very well, very well. And have we any ebony to sell?

6
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:34,160
 Truth to tell, our business has done well lately. Nearly all of our slaves have been sold.

7
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:36,340
 It's not sure. We can supply all they'd like.

8
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:38,180
 Then go to work. What are you waiting for?

9
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:43,150
 Send our hunters out to round up some slaves, and while you're at it, go and capture an entire village.

10
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:48,510
 Yes, and loving is giving to your fellow man.

11
00:00:48,750 --> 00:00:53,170
 That is what I learned one day from a wise old man whose skin was as dark as ours.

12
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:54,930
 Why are you telling us this, Father?

13
00:00:55,130 --> 00:00:57,190
 Don't all people have dark skin like us?

14
00:00:57,290 --> 00:00:58,370
 Oh, no, my child.

15
00:00:58,690 --> 00:01:00,950
 Because there are people with light skin.

16
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:03,660
 I hope you never meet one of them.

17
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:06,280
 As our traditional tales say, they're evil.

18
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:08,880
 They sow unhappiness, stay out of their path.

19
00:01:13,810 --> 00:01:20,220
 Must clear to have good planting land.

20
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:21,400
 All right.

21
00:01:28,110 --> 00:01:55,090
 Somebody shout.

22
00:01:55,090 --> 00:02:52,950
 Lisa Monksage says that a chief of a coastal land has sent out warriors to capture us and our neighbors.

23
00:02:53,270 --> 00:02:54,630
 All of you be on your guard.

24
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:56,240
 What did we do to them?

25
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:57,240
 Doesn't that mean war?

26
00:02:57,770 --> 00:02:59,450
 Why are they doing this to us?

27
00:02:59,750 --> 00:03:02,770
 They want to sell us to white men who would make us their slaves.

28
00:03:02,850 --> 00:03:03,510
 That's the reason.

29
00:03:04,110 --> 00:03:05,530
 But we won't let them do it.

30
00:03:05,870 --> 00:03:06,570
 We won't.

31
00:03:06,690 --> 00:03:07,690
 We'll outwit them.

32
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:09,250
 And here is what we're going to do.

33
00:03:38,510 --> 00:03:59,350
 Right all, they won't go away.

34
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:02,720
 The time has come to use maximum force and this will be our last chance.

35
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:27,350
 Place to get water.

36
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:34,110
 Strongest.

37
00:06:35,470 --> 00:06:44,960
 Get a good for a little rest.

38
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:56,300
 Go listen Beth, I'll buy her, say a bag of robber.

39
00:06:56,300 --> 00:07:13,310
 It's a large sack of pep, tape, a sack of ivory, how's that?

40
00:07:13,310 --> 00:07:19,500
 Those two I will buy then, and that one. How much for the whole set?

41
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:24,880
 No dear, I'm selling him to the white man and he's going to cost them plenty, I guarantee you that.

42
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:36,130
 Go on, bunch of good fun. The more muscle on you, the more you're worth.

43
00:07:36,130 --> 00:07:37,440
 I can't go on.

44
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:44,000
 I'll carry her bag? Me, I'm strong, I'll carry it. If you want to, you carry it.

45
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,670
 What's that? It's the sea, the ocean.

46
00:07:48,670 --> 00:07:51,290
 See? Look how beautiful it is.

47
00:07:51,290 --> 00:07:55,290
 And that is my master's palace. He's very rich. Very rich and powerful.

48
00:07:55,290 --> 00:07:58,380
 You're going to help him be even richer and more powerful.

49
00:07:58,380 --> 00:08:21,340
 Soon it took you long enough.

50
00:08:21,340 --> 00:08:24,850
 But capturing all those excellent slaves took more time than...

51
00:08:24,850 --> 00:08:26,850
 Did you bring back many?

52
00:08:26,850 --> 00:08:30,850
 I got you first-class quality. You'll be pleased. You never saw such quality.

53
00:08:30,850 --> 00:08:32,850
 There are how many in total?

54
00:08:32,850 --> 00:08:35,730
 Many.

55
00:08:35,730 --> 00:08:39,720
 Thought I told you I wanted a whole village. You know what a village is, you village idiot?

56
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:42,670
 Was it how you saw such a tiny village?

57
00:08:42,670 --> 00:08:48,400
 I am going to sell you right now to the White, this idiot!

58
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:59,160
 Great Captain, I am the Great Yavogan of our Great King. I come in his name.

59
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:01,490
 Say you are the one.

60
00:09:01,490 --> 00:09:07,170
 The Great Yavogan, the Minister of Foreign Trade. If the Captain would care to follow me?

61
00:09:07,170 --> 00:09:15,570
 Capitaine Dupandor, subject of His Majesty the King of France. We look alike.

62
00:09:15,570 --> 00:09:18,720
 Huh? Yes, we look alike.

63
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:25,570
 We'll permit a few gifts for Your Majesty.

64
00:09:25,570 --> 00:09:28,320
 I'll talk business if you want.

65
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:34,400
 You will purchase all you've got of rubber, an ivory, pepper, and especially all the ebony wood you care to sell.

66
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:37,040
 Tell me how many you've got.

67
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:38,420
 Tell me how much you'll pay.

68
00:09:38,420 --> 00:09:47,420
 In exchange, I'm offering you a barrel of excellent brandy, cloth, barrels of gunpowder, rifles, shells, strings of pearls, and one or two special gifts.

69
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:53,840
 It's worth a fortune, your majesty, it's worth a fortune.

70
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:59,500
 Now, your majesty, show me your ebony. Five rifles, two barrels of powder.

71
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:01,500
 I'll give you six rifles, three barrels.

72
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:03,850
 Eight rivels, three barrels.

73
00:10:03,850 --> 00:10:04,990
 Done.

74
00:10:04,990 --> 00:10:09,100
 Laura!

75
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:13,170
 Bolts of cloth plus two barrels of liquor.

76
00:10:13,170 --> 00:10:15,930
 I say two bolts of cloth plus three barrels of brandy.

77
00:10:15,930 --> 00:10:18,900
 Three bolts of cloth.

78
00:10:18,900 --> 00:10:20,890
 I say three bolts and four barrels.

79
00:10:20,890 --> 00:10:29,120
 Sure are ugly, they look pale like they're sick.

80
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:32,060
 And they are evil. I hope the Dutchman buys us.

81
00:10:32,060 --> 00:10:32,830
 Five!

82
00:10:32,830 --> 00:10:35,520
 Plus one.

83
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:47,010
 Where do you think we're going?

84
00:10:47,010 --> 00:10:49,170
 I don't know. Maybe to France?

85
00:10:49,170 --> 00:10:51,960
 Oh no, my children.

86
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:54,480
 No, we're not going to France, but America.

87
00:10:54,480 --> 00:10:56,350
 America? Where in the world is that?

88
00:10:56,350 --> 00:10:59,010
 It's far away, it's on the other side of the world.

89
00:10:59,010 --> 00:11:02,110
 America yet?

90
00:11:02,110 --> 00:11:03,650
 No, no, no, no.

91
00:11:03,650 --> 00:11:07,520
 We are going up and down the African coast until the ship is full to breaking.

92
00:11:07,520 --> 00:11:09,570
 What do you know about all this anyway?

93
00:11:09,570 --> 00:11:13,480
 In my village, I was a wise man, a medicine man.

94
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:15,490
 I was able to foresee many things.

95
00:11:15,490 --> 00:11:18,780
 That's funny, but you remind me of somebody, but who?

96
00:11:18,780 --> 00:11:31,840
 Now we are off to the Americas.

97
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:45,530
 Pirate, is it you, Pirate?

98
00:11:45,530 --> 00:11:48,800
 You knew how him?

99
00:11:48,800 --> 00:12:15,010
 Now I lighten me off.

100
00:12:15,010 --> 00:12:23,260
 Let's go.

101
00:12:23,260 --> 00:12:33,050
 Whim, let's dive over.

102
00:12:33,050 --> 00:12:37,310
 No, no lad, no, don't try it. You wouldn't have a chance.

103
00:12:37,310 --> 00:12:57,890
 What's going on, sheep?

104
00:12:57,890 --> 00:13:22,330
 Siplien, hold and chain him!

105
00:13:22,330 --> 00:13:25,600
 Who do you think you are, huh?

106
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:26,600
 It gets cut!

107
00:13:26,600 --> 00:13:31,040
 I warn you, my beard has magic powers, you'd better be careful!

108
00:13:31,040 --> 00:13:34,040
 If you cut it, you will be cursed!

109
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:45,410
 Yeah, I'm alright, but I'm sure hungry.

110
00:13:45,410 --> 00:13:47,590
 Thanks!

111
00:13:47,590 --> 00:14:09,210
 Oh, I'm alright, a little seasick, I have to get used to it.

112
00:14:09,210 --> 00:14:11,210
 Say, Captain, our cargo's getting weaker.

113
00:14:11,210 --> 00:14:15,270
 Oh, that's bad for business, we'll lose money, we have to do something, right?

114
00:14:15,270 --> 00:14:16,230
 Uh-huh.

115
00:14:16,230 --> 00:14:18,840
 Give them some exercise. That'll do it.

116
00:14:18,840 --> 00:14:22,840
 Good idea, Captain, and I know just the sort of exercise that'll be good for 'em.

117
00:14:22,840 --> 00:14:27,210
 You, come over here. Come on, play a lively tune.

118
00:14:27,210 --> 00:14:30,550
 You bring up all my slaves. Up, go on deck, and quick!

119
00:14:30,550 --> 00:15:02,050
 Think we have a chance of getting away?

120
00:15:02,050 --> 00:15:05,810
 We outnumber them. In a fight, we'd be sure to come out on top.

121
00:15:05,810 --> 00:15:08,810
 Some of our brothers and other ships tried just that,

122
00:15:08,810 --> 00:15:14,210
 but just like us, they couldn't sail the white man's ship.

123
00:15:14,210 --> 00:15:17,210
 Courage died with them. Did them no good. They perished.

124
00:15:17,210 --> 00:15:21,680
 It's a slave ship and it's in mutiny. Take a look.

125
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:24,530
 It's a Dutch one.

126
00:15:24,530 --> 00:15:25,530
 A Dutch ship.

127
00:15:25,530 --> 00:15:26,530
 Then who cares?

128
00:15:26,530 --> 00:15:58,030
 Shipments from Africa are rare?

129
00:15:58,030 --> 00:16:02,030
 Yes, only Africans can work well in this hot climate. None of us can.

130
00:16:02,030 --> 00:16:38,660
 Yes, and I declare this day is a real scorcher.

131
00:16:38,660 --> 00:16:41,160
 Just like that.

132
00:16:41,160 --> 00:16:44,160
 In a way, you could say what you're pulling off is a white wasp.

133
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:46,160
 And you could use a freshening up.

134
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:50,020
 I could. I don't look good. Hey, no, what's the point?

135
00:16:50,020 --> 00:16:54,910
 No, no, no, believe me, your oil is no good at all.

136
00:16:54,910 --> 00:17:24,990
 it's worthless but this one does wonders give it to me right now oh beer what is it about to begin

137
00:17:24,990 --> 00:17:36,650
 no unsold goods all goods will be sold in lots naturally no unsold merchandise well it's just

138
00:17:36,650 --> 00:18:08,720
 good business what am i dead and even tobacco we tried indians even poor whites but no nothing can

139
00:18:08,720 --> 00:18:14,500
 replace a black when it comes to hard work all right who's spinning first bed too old no good

140
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:25,730
 for anything. What? What? You have the merchandise first quality. Come and see. Yeah, these are too

141
00:18:25,730 --> 00:18:32,280
 skinny. You can't get any work out of them. 9th lot. 8 high quality slaves. Too many women. No

142
00:18:32,280 --> 00:18:40,000
 sale. Lot 22. Superb quality. 3 fine slaves. Who's first? Just a minute. I'm in this lot too.

143
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:48,220
 Where did you come from? Maybe down in the hole. Where do you think? But without Methuselah. I'm

144
00:18:48,220 --> 00:19:28,500
 sorry, but the deal is the whole lot on nothing. Gentleman, he needs a second lesser officially for

145
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:32,530
 You'll be second in charge and have a brand new whip, and you can use it all you want.

146
00:19:32,530 --> 00:20:45,180
 Please, sir, you have this man. He just got 200 lashes.

147
00:20:45,180 --> 00:20:53,950
 If he comes away, hear him ring them bells, and he'll be snagged in the branches.

148
00:20:53,950 --> 00:21:06,900
 Child, you'll need all your courage. A few hours ago, they took your little boy away.

149
00:21:06,900 --> 00:21:12,750
 What possible interest would I have in them? They're too little to work. Look at them!

150
00:21:12,750 --> 00:21:17,750
 Look, you know, now it's illegal to import slaves. I'll sell you these at a good price.

151
00:21:17,750 --> 00:21:20,750
 And in a few years, they'll grow up and work hard. You can't go wrong.

152
00:21:20,750 --> 00:21:21,450
 Can't go wrong.

153
00:21:26,610 --> 00:21:30,550
 It was a shame, taking children from their mothers.

154
00:21:32,900 --> 00:21:35,780
 Goodness, I think I know you.

155
00:21:35,820 --> 00:21:36,580
 What's your name?

156
00:21:37,490 --> 00:21:39,070
 Pierrot.

157
00:21:41,790 --> 00:21:45,500
 That I don't charge a lot.

158
00:21:48,750 --> 00:21:54,370
 When I think of a little boy, poor Pierrot, my God.

159
00:21:54,850 --> 00:21:55,990
 Don't cry, my child.

160
00:21:56,070 --> 00:21:56,910
 Don't torment yourself.

161
00:21:57,010 --> 00:21:57,790
 I saw Pierrette.

162
00:21:58,100 --> 00:22:01,320
 She's with Mokhtar in a beautiful coffee plantation.

163
00:22:02,320 --> 00:22:03,980
 They don't work in the fields.

164
00:22:04,100 --> 00:22:05,320
 They're servants in the mansion.

165
00:22:05,640 --> 00:22:07,020
 And they're well treated.

166
00:22:07,900 --> 00:22:09,630
 Children are cared for.

167
00:22:09,790 --> 00:22:10,430
 Yours, too.

168
00:22:10,590 --> 00:22:11,950
 And they all play together.

169
00:22:11,950 --> 00:22:14,140
 You can believe in my visions.

170
00:22:14,280 --> 00:22:14,910
 You know that.

171
00:22:16,300 --> 00:22:18,530
 I just want to get away from this place.

172
00:22:18,630 --> 00:22:20,650
 I want to go before they steal all our children.

173
00:22:20,930 --> 00:22:22,050
 But where would we go?

174
00:22:22,390 --> 00:22:25,970
 I heard that up north, in those states, there's no slaves.

175
00:22:26,150 --> 00:22:27,570
 But can we really trust them?

176
00:22:28,950 --> 00:22:30,790
 Let's go up north.

177
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:33,320
 Our children will be free.

178
00:22:33,670 --> 00:22:34,330
 Be free.

179
00:22:37,160 --> 00:22:39,530
 Things arranged.

180
00:22:40,220 --> 00:22:41,720
 So, a little discussion, huh?

181
00:22:42,080 --> 00:22:43,240
 Here, you work.

182
00:22:43,320 --> 00:22:43,980
 You don't talk.

183
00:22:44,160 --> 00:22:46,370
 It will weigh what you say.

184
00:22:46,370 --> 00:22:48,760
 For 200 pounds, you'll be punished.

185
00:22:49,020 --> 00:22:56,080
 These days, your whip will be turned against you.

186
00:22:56,680 --> 00:22:58,250
 You sure you won't go with us?

187
00:22:58,510 --> 00:22:59,710
 You won't change your mind?

188
00:23:00,150 --> 00:23:04,170
 I'd better stay with the others. I'll do what I can for them, as little as it may be.

189
00:23:04,250 --> 00:23:06,740
 You go on north, Third Hope.

190
00:23:07,050 --> 00:23:11,190
 Your route will be difficult and dangerous, and even if you make it,

191
00:23:11,270 --> 00:23:15,930
 you will continue to suffer and struggle, and that seems to be our fate.

192
00:23:16,250 --> 00:23:19,950
 But I know that one day your children will be free and respected.

193
00:23:20,110 --> 00:23:23,550
 Who knows, one day even state governors, and even the president.

194
00:23:23,550 --> 00:23:27,630
 It required several decades before the slave trade was finally abolished,

195
00:23:27,730 --> 00:23:29,710
 and no more slave ships crossed the ocean.

196
00:23:30,310 --> 00:23:32,910
 The French went on until 1831,

197
00:23:33,350 --> 00:23:36,270
 and slavery wasn't totally abolished in the United States

198
00:23:36,270 --> 00:23:37,790
 until after the Civil War.

199
00:23:38,590 --> 00:23:41,490
 10 to 14 million people were taken from Africa

200
00:23:41,490 --> 00:23:44,610
 and deported to the new world, the Americas.

